text:
The Two Parts of Every Shiny App
Every Shiny app — no matter how simple or complex — is built from exactly two components that work together like a two-way conversation.

The UI (User Interface) — The Front End
The UI is everything the user sees: the page layout, the buttons, dropdown menus, sliders, and the space where plots and tables appear. You define it using R functions like fluidPage(), sidebarLayout(), selectInput(), and plotOutput(). Think of the UI as the scaffolding — it positions your outputs and makes the app look good, but it doesn’t do any of the calculations itself.
ui &lt;- fluidPage(
  titlePanel("My App"),
  sidebarLayout(
    sidebarPanel(
      selectInput("metric", "Choose a Metric:", 
                  choices = c("Attendance", "Daily Average"))
    ),
    mainPanel(
      plotOutput("mainPlot")
    )
  )
)


The Server — The Back End
The server is where your R logic lives. It takes whatever input the user selected in the UI, runs your code, and sends the result (a plot, a table, a number) back to the screen. The server function always takes two arguments: input and output.
server &lt;- function(input, output) {
  output$mainPlot &lt;- renderPlot({
    # This code re-runs every time input$metric changes
    hist(rnorm(100), main = paste("Trend for", input$metric))
  })
}
Key rule: The UI describes what exists on the page. The server describes what those things do. Notice how input$metric in the server matches the inputId "metric" defined in selectInput() in the UI. These IDs must match exactly — that is how the two halves talk to each other.

Putting It Together — The Full app.R Structure
A standard single-file Shiny app lives in a file called app.R and always ends with shinyApp() to launch it:
library(shiny)

# 1. Define the UI
ui &lt;- fluidPage(
  titlePanel("Our First Shiny App"),
  sidebarLayout(
    sidebarPanel(
      selectInput("variable", "Choose a Metric:", 
                  choices = c("Sales", "Conversions"))
    ),
    mainPanel(
      plotOutput("mainPlot")
    )
  )
)

# 2. Define the server logic
server &lt;- function(input, output) {
  output$mainPlot &lt;- renderPlot({
    hist(rnorm(100), main = paste("Trend for", input$variable))
  })
}

# 3. Launch the app
shinyApp(ui = ui, server = server)
To run it in RStudio, click the Run App button in the top-right of the editor, or press Cmd/Ctrl + Shift + Enter. When the app launches, RStudio will display a local URL like 127.0.0.1:3827 — this means “my computer, port 3827.” You can open that URL in any browser to see another copy of the app. Note that while the app is running, R is busy and the console is blocked. To stop it, click the red stop sign in the console or press Esc.
